x)+<gradient(W_y)(x),G_rep(x)>_E-f_rep(x); this notation abbreviates the exact displayed expression.\n(1) For every v in domain(D.closure),\n  epsilon*<u,v>_{L2(S(y);R)}+<D.closure(u),D.closure(v)>_{L2(S(y);E)}=<f,v>_{L2(S(y);R)}.\n(2) u_rep and G_rep are locally integrable with respect to canonical Lebesgue volume.\n(3) For every C1 compactly supported psi:E->R and every direction v in E, both x->psi(x)*<G_rep(x),v>_E and x->u_rep(x)*(fderiv R psi x v) are Lebesgue integrable, and\n  integral psi(x)*<G_rep(x),v>_E dx = - integral u_rep(x)*(fderiv R psi x v) dx.\n(4) x->||r(x)||^2 is locally Lebesgue integrable, with the real scalar norm in this squared expression.\n(5) For every compact set K contained in E, u_rep, G_rep, and r each satisfy MemLp with exponent 2 and the actual restricted measure volume.restrict K. Each assertion includes a.e. strong measurability and finite L2 norm with respect to that restricted measure.\n(6) For every C2 compactly supported phi:E->R, both x->u_rep(x)*Laplacian.laplacian(phi)(x) and x->r(x)*phi(x) are Lebesgue integrable, and\n  integral u_rep(x)*Laplacian.laplacian(phi)(x) dx = integral r(x)*phi(x) dx.\nHere the test-function Laplacian is the actual finite-basis trace of its derivative. The distribution equation tests u_rep against this C2 test Laplacian; it makes no pointwise total-Laplacian assertion about u_rep. The Hilbert variational inner products use S(y), while all local integrability and distribution integrals use canonical Lebesgue volume. One u and its actual output G serve all variational v, all C1 directional tests, every compact K, and all C2 distribution tests.\nOne pair R,S works for every y. For each y one D is chosen before every positive epsilon and every L2 input f, and the same D works for all of them; it is not chosen anew for each epsilon or f. The density and operator conclusions hold for all y, not merely almost every y. The conditional-kernel property itself has the usual disintegration scope and does not make a claim about every possible version on exceptional y. The exact r is epsilon*u_rep+<gradient(W_y),G_rep>-f_rep. No classical Laplacian or H2 conclusion for u, core theorem, measurable y-dependent operator/solution selection, kernel invariance, or quantitative solution bound is asserted.",
